<file path="docs/adr/0008-make-desktop-application-the-primary-entry.md">
# Make the desktop application the primary product entry

Mellowday will use an Electron and TypeScript Desktop Shell as its primary Windows entry, presenting the existing Web App and starting the packaged Python backend as a managed sidecar. The shell owns Windows lifecycle, single-instance behavior, system tray presence, startup registration, desktop notifications, and backend recovery, while browser mode remains available and Agent Core, Personal Assistant, and Web App remain independent of Electron. This reuses the proven web product while choosing a mature desktop lifecycle over a pywebview composition, a Rust-and-Python Tauri stack, or a WinUI rewrite.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0009-use-portable-application-managed-provider-key-encryption.md">
# Use portable application-managed Provider key encryption

Mellowday encrypts Provider API keys before local persistence using application-managed random key material, with no master password and no Windows user or device binding. Portable data and backups include the material needed by Mellowday to decrypt those credentials on another machine. This deliberately prioritizes transparent use and portability: it prevents direct plaintext inspection of SQLite and backup contents, but it does not claim protection against an attacker who possesses the complete application and data or reverse-engineers the application.
</file>

