<file path="docs/adr/0001-single-user-local-first.md">
# Use a single-user, local-first product model

The assistant is self-hosted for one user, and persona, memories, conversations, life records, settings, and audit data remain local. Model providers are replaceable inference adapters because coupling personal data ownership to a hosted provider would weaken portability and user control; multi-user and tenant concerns are deliberately excluded.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0002-separate-core-assistant-and-web.md">
# Separate Agent Core, Personal Assistant, and Web App

The product is divided into Agent Core, Personal Assistant, and Web App modules. This keeps the reusable model and tool loop independent from personal-assistant behaviour and browser delivery, while allowing the same Personal Assistant management features to appear inside one integrated web experience rather than a separate administration product.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0003-persona-applies-only-to-chat.md">
# Apply persona only to chat content

Persona shapes every conversational message, including clarifications, failures, and refusals, but never shapes Settings, permissions, logs, diagnostics, stored records, or audit output. This preserves a convincing companion in conversation without allowing character performance to blur operational truth.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0004-memory-is-not-a-life-record.md">
# Keep Memory separate from life records

Memory stores durable preferences, facts, and important context about the user, while Tasks, Reminders, Calendar Events, and Notes remain separate sources of truth. Treating all of them as generic memories would make deadlines, completion, notification, and correction behaviour ambiguous and difficult to manage reliably.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0005-use-natural-intent-for-reversible-actions.md">
# Use natural intent for reversible internal actions

A clear natural-language request authorizes a reversible internal action without a second confirmation; ambiguous requests receive a Persona-consistent clarification, and only high-risk or irreversible actions require explicit confirmation. This avoids robotic confirmation loops while preserving a deliberate safety seam for consequential actions.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0006-keep-proactive-chat-read-only.md">
# Keep proactive chat read-only

Proactive Chat may use bounded scheduling, Persona, relevant Memory, and recent context to decide whether to send a companion message, but it cannot invoke write tools or change stored data. This preserves spontaneous companionship without turning background model judgment into unsupervised action.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0007-extract-reference-without-runtime-dependency.md">
# Extract the reference implementation without a runtime dependency

The new root project uses `chatbot/` as a read-only source of implementation evidence. It extracts or adapts the proven Agent Core and generic backend-management behavior behind new, product-neutral interfaces, while excluding QQ/OneBot integration and bundled tool and Skill implementations.

The new runtime must not import from, package, or otherwise depend on the reference tree. This keeps the resulting assistant independently buildable, allows the reference clone to remain excluded from Git, and prevents QQ-specific assumptions from leaking into the new architecture.
</file>

